WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.283 --> 00:00:05.921
- [Crys] I think that in
the skateboarding culture

00:00:05.921 --> 00:00:10.403
you get a lot of people in
general that are different.

00:00:10.403 --> 00:00:13.042
These are just kids that want a place,

00:00:13.042 --> 00:00:15.259
and want to be in their own place

00:00:15.259 --> 00:00:18.759
and not doing what everybody else is doing.

00:00:21.704 --> 00:00:24.024
- [Voiceover] Crys Worley
is the founder of A.Skate,

00:00:24.024 --> 00:00:26.442
a non-profit that teaches kids with autism

00:00:26.442 --> 00:00:28.344
how to skateboard.

00:00:28.344 --> 00:00:32.024
- Autism has a lot of aspects to it.

00:00:32.024 --> 00:00:35.396
Most of the kids need
occupational therapy of some form.

00:00:35.396 --> 00:00:39.063
Skateboarding is similar
to other therapies.

00:00:40.365 --> 00:00:43.384
They're learning how to
control their body in motion,

00:00:43.384 --> 00:00:46.821
and it just stimulates parts of the brain

00:00:46.821 --> 00:00:50.988
that need to be stimulated in
order to do things in life,

00:00:52.104 --> 00:00:56.271
in order to have conversations,
in order to pay attention.

00:00:58.403 --> 00:01:01.521
I have a son who will soon be 14.

00:01:01.521 --> 00:01:04.299
His name is Sasha, and he
was diagnosed with autism

00:01:04.299 --> 00:01:06.163
at 22 months old.

00:01:06.163 --> 00:01:08.520
I bought him his first skateboard

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:10.643
when he was right at five years old.

00:01:10.643 --> 00:01:12.024
His brother was three.

00:01:12.024 --> 00:01:15.681
It was really difficult for them to just

00:01:15.681 --> 00:01:16.840
come together and play.

00:01:16.840 --> 00:01:18.481
There was no play.

00:01:18.481 --> 00:01:20.664
Somehow the movement of the skateboard

00:01:20.664 --> 00:01:24.831
just made them focused,
and they would just skate,

00:01:26.323 --> 00:01:29.405
and I want to do that for other families.

00:01:29.405 --> 00:01:31.099
- [Voiceover] Crys started
A.Skate eight years ago

00:01:31.099 --> 00:01:34.163
in a church parking lot
in Birmingham, Alabama.

00:01:34.163 --> 00:01:37.716
It's since expanded across
the US and internationally.

00:01:37.716 --> 00:01:39.177
This year they were invited to participate

00:01:39.177 --> 00:01:42.561
in the Dew Tour, a pro
skating contest in California

00:01:42.561 --> 00:01:45.640
where kids were paired with
professional skateboarders.

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:48.904
- I've known Sasha since I
got involved with A.Skate

00:01:48.904 --> 00:01:50.457
about five years ago.

00:01:50.457 --> 00:01:54.664
The joy that I see that it brings to kids,

00:01:54.664 --> 00:01:57.200
like that's where I know that this is

00:01:57.200 --> 00:01:59.181
what I'm supposed to be doing.

00:01:59.181 --> 00:02:02.083
The skateboarding culture
is accepting of everyone,

00:02:02.083 --> 00:02:04.125
you know, spectrum or not spectrum,

00:02:04.125 --> 00:02:07.520
no one cares what color, type, gender,

00:02:07.520 --> 00:02:10.723
whatever it is like if you
can skate, you can skate,

00:02:10.723 --> 00:02:13.123
and we got love for you.

00:02:13.123 --> 00:02:16.373
- [Crys] It's really
incredible to see kids change

00:02:16.373 --> 00:02:18.596
through our program.

00:02:18.596 --> 00:02:21.513
I see families just see their child

00:02:22.554 --> 00:02:25.021
who is now capable of something,

00:02:25.021 --> 00:02:27.560
and they didn't know it.

00:02:27.560 --> 00:02:31.727
In a way, forming A.Skate is
like a love letter to Sasha,

00:02:32.659 --> 00:02:36.826
and he may never understand
that or know it, but I do.

